  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1130 AM CDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nLate morning water vapor satellite imagery and RAP upper air \nanalysis reveal a modestly strong ridge over the Desert Southwest \nhas begun to build northward toward the Four Corners region. At the \nsurface, weak high pressure is spread out across nearly all areas \neast of the Rockies, resulting in a nebulous pressure gradient over \nthe central plains. Despite 500-mb height rises above southwest KS, \nevaporative cooling courtesy of last night's thunderstorms will help \nshave a few degrees off afternoon temperatures today with highs in \nthe mid to upper 90s. A few HREF members suggest a stray \nthunderstorm or two could find its way into our northwestern zones \nlater this afternoon, however strong DNVA ahead of the upper ridge \ncasts considerable doubt on this scenario. Tonight, clear skies and \nlight winds should support Saturday morning lows dropping into the \nupper 60s to mid 70s.\n\nDaytime Saturday, short range ensembles agree the upper level ridge \nwill inch from the Four Corners region eastward to near the central \nRockies/Plains. The following increase in subsidence will allow 850-\nmb temperatures to rise into the 30-35 degrees C range, which will \ntranslate to afternoon highs in the 99-105 degree F range at the \nsurface. \n\nSunday through the end of the period, medium range ensembles agree \nthe upper level ridge will stay virtually stationary over the \ncentral Rockies/plains, supporting a prolonged dry, very hot stretch \nfor southwest KS. Monday appears likely to be the hottest day of the \nyear so far as afternoon highs surge into the 105-110 degree range. \nRecord highs of 109 in Dodge City and 108 in Garden City will be \nthreatened, and multiple days of Heat Advisories, and perhaps an \nExtreme Heat Warning, will be forthcoming.",
